linux-rockchip64-ysp (6.18.40+rk3588av1fwport20260729-0ubuntu1~rk1) resolute; urgency=high

  * Provenance repair: re-cut the upstream source from the forward-port
    worktree. The 20260725 orig was accidentally a rewrite-composite snapshot
    of the shared Armbian worktree, not the validated forward-port series: its
    drivers/iommu/rockchip-iommu.c carried the rewrite branch's hardened
    rockchip_iommu_set_fault_handler(), whose clear path calls
    platform_get_irq() (sleeping irq_domain mutex) and synchronize_irq().
  * That tail is fatal with the vendor MPP driver, which clears the fault
    handler from its job ISR under a spinlock: on 2026-07-29 08:01 PDT the
    ~rk2 kernel panicked from the idle task mid RDP session ("BUG: scheduling
    while atomic: swapper/0", branch to non-executable memory, "Attempted to
    kill the idle task!"; full ramoops record). Any codec job could reproduce
    it under irq_domain mutex contention.
  * The forward-port series never contained the hardened setter, so this
    upload restores the intended atomic-safe pointer-swap implementation by
    provenance alone; no forward-port code change is introduced.
  * The source exporter now drops drivers/video/rockchip/{mpp,rga}-rewrite/
    paths from worktree exports so composite build leftovers can no longer
    reach this package.
  * Config is unchanged from ~rk2 (CONFIG_DMABUF_DEBUG and the three baseline
    debug options remain off).

 -- Yi Ding <yi.s.ding@gmail.com>  Wed, 29 Jul 2026 09:10:00 -0700

linux-rockchip64-ysp (6.18.40+rk3588av1fwport20260725-0ubuntu1~rk2) resolute; urgency=high

  * Config-only release: the upstream source is byte-identical to ~rk1, and the
    source package is built from that release's own orig tarball. Four config
    symbols change, all of them debug options being switched off.
  * Disable CONFIG_DMABUF_DEBUG. This is the functional fix.
  * Fixes the deterministic oops on GNOME Remote Desktop's first RKMPP H.264
    frame, which left the board with no login video at all — 3/3 boots, and
    seven times within a single boot.
  * CONFIG_DMABUF_DEBUG compiles in mangle_sg_table() (drivers/dma-buf/dma-buf.c
    ~:831), which XORs every scatterlist page_link with ~0xffUL for the interval
    an attachment is mapped, to catch importers that peek at the underlying
    struct page. The core applies it to the exporter's own per-attachment table
    in dma_buf_map_attachment() and undoes it only around the unmap callback.
    system_heap_dma_buf_end_cpu_access() is reached straight from
    DMA_BUF_IOCTL_SYNC, outside that bracket, so it syncs the scrambled table
    and iommu_dma_sync_sg_for_device() faults dereferencing sg_phys() on a
    non-coherent device. All 8/8 recorded fault addresses invert through that
    XOR to valid in-RAM page frames, and the option correlates 4/4 with
    reproduction across every 6.18.40 kernel on the board.
  * The option was never chosen: Kconfig marks it "default y if DMA_API_DEBUG",
    so it arrived automatically when DMA_API_DEBUG was enabled. DMA_API_DEBUG
    itself stays on — it does not gate the mangle, and the explicit
    "is not set" line overrides the default. It has exactly one reverse
    dependency tree-wide, dma-buf.c:833, so nothing else changes.
  * Not a Rockchip or vendor-driver defect. The underlying incompatibility is
    upstream and general: any dma-heap implementing begin/end_cpu_access this
    way is an unconditional oops under the option on a non-coherent device.
  * Disable the three remaining debug options this config enabled on code that
    Armbian's own kernel also ships, bringing the production config back in line
    with the stock 26.5.1 baseline:
    - CONFIG_DMA_API_DEBUG. Besides being the "default y if" parent that armed
      DMABUF_DEBUG, it preallocated a debug pool sized by the shared
      dma_debug_entries=2097152 boot argument -- 2097152 entries at 128 B each,
      256 MiB of permanently resident kernel memory, against an 8 MiB default --
      and cost a hash lookup plus spinlock on every DMA map, unmap and sync, on
      a board whose video pipeline performs thousands per second. Turning it off
      also makes that boot argument inert, so the memory is reclaimed without
      editing the shared boot environment.
    - CONFIG_IOMMU_DEBUGFS. Inspection nodes only; the AMD/Intel consumers are
      x86-only, so nothing on arm64 depends on it.
    - CONFIG_KALLSYMS_ALL. Data symbols in the kallsyms table, roughly a
      megabyte or two. LIVEPATCH is disabled and no enabled symbol selects it,
      so nothing else changes; function symbols remain, so oops backtraces
      decode as before.
    The RGA and MPP debug facets stay enabled: they are sub-options of drivers
    Armbian does not ship at all, so there is no stock baseline to match, and
    their verbosity controls default to zero.

 -- Yi Ding <yi.s.ding@gmail.com>  Tue, 28 Jul 2026 15:24:00 -0700

